Based on the provided context, here is a Semgrep rule that matches the vulnerable code structure:

```yaml
rules:
- id: "vuln-htmlunit-940dc7fd"
  pattern: $parent = startingNode.getParentNode();
          while $parent != null && $parent != DomNode.this {
            $next = $parent.getNextSibling();
            while $next != null && !isAccepted($next) {
              $next = $next.getNextSibling();
            }
            if $next != null {
              return $next;
            }
            $parent = $parent.getParentNode();
          }
  languages: ["java"]
  message: |
    Potential null pointer exception vulnerability. 
    The code is trying to get the next sibling of a node. If the parent node is null or the parent node is the current node itself, it returns null. 
    If the next sibling is not accepted, it continues to find the next sibling. 
    If no next sibling is found, it tries to find the next sibling in the parent's parent node. 
    This could lead to a NullPointerException if the structure of the tree is not properly maintained.
  severity: ERROR
  metadata:
    source-url: github.com/HtmlUnit/htmlunit/commit/940dc7fd8af9f46ca448c1e548b8f6d064a64290
    category: security
    cwe: [relevant CWE number]
    owasp: [relevant OWASP category]
    references: [links to documentation or standards]
    technology: [java]
```

Please replace `[relevant CWE number]`, `[relevant OWASP category]`, `[links to documentation or standards]` with the actual values.

This rule will match any code that has the same structure as the vulnerable code. It's important to note that this rule will not catch all potential null pointer exceptions, but it will catch the ones that are caused by the structure of the code that matches the pattern.
